Reading Guide - Graduation Project

Designing a User‑Friendly Template System for Campaign Landing Pages in Delta’s Custom CMS



1. Background

1.1 Project summary

In this graduation project I work with the digital and marketing teams at Delta Electronics EMEA to improve the way campaign landing pages are created and maintained in the company’s custom CMS. At the moment, many landing pages are still set up on a case‑by‑case basis, with manual adjustments and one‑off solutions by the front‑end and digital team. This leads to differences in structure and quality between campaigns and makes marketers dependent on developers for relatively small changes.

For many marketers, the current templates and configuration screens do not feel intuitive or self‑service. They often struggle to translate their campaign ideas into consistent pages without asking for technical help. Because the pain points and constraints around template reuse and configuration have not yet been mapped in a structured way, Delta does not have a user‑friendly template system that allows marketing teams to assemble campaign landing pages from reusable building blocks in a predictable way.

In this project I design, implement and evaluate a template system within the existing CMS that is easier for marketers to configure and that helps create consistent, well‑structured campaign landing pages using reusable components. The work combines UX research, design thinking and front‑end implementation.

1.2 Problem statement

Marketing teams at Delta EMEA frequently experience difficulties when they create campaign landing pages without direct support from developers. Existing templates and configuration options are not always clear or flexible enough, which results in ad‑hoc solutions, extra implementation effort and pages that differ in structure and quality.

A more user‑friendly template system is needed: one that offers reusable components, clearer configuration flows and better support for marketers, while remaining maintainable for the digital/front‑end team.

1.3 How the project is organised

I describe this project using a Design Thinking process with six steps: Empathize, Define, Ideate, Prototype, Test and Implement. Each step combines HBO‑ICT research methods with concrete design and implementation work in the CMS.





Empathize – Understand how campaign landing pages are currently created in the CMS and what problems marketers and developers encounter.



Define – Translate these findings into clear goals, constraints and requirements for a template system.



Ideate – Explore and compare concepts for templates, components, configuration flows and potential AI support.



Prototype – Design and build a first working version of the template system in the CMS.



Test – Evaluate the new system and, where possible, an AI concept with stakeholders through tasks, feedback and before/after comparisons.



Implement – Refine and complete the template system in the CMS based on usability‑testing feedback, and deliver a final, documented version that is ready for use in real campaigns.

The project runs over approximately twenty weeks. I use a Kanban board (To Do / In Progress / Done) for day‑to‑day planning and for alignment with my company mentor, the front‑end developer and my Fontys coach. Testing and quality checks for layout, responsiveness, accessibility and maintainability are integrated into the implementation work rather than treated as a separate phase.

