Results
Empathize yields a structured overview of how campaign landing pages are currently created, which roles are involved when, and which tools and templates are used in each step. Across the five case studies, interviews, survey and observations, recurring pain points emerge around efficiency (extra manual work and rework), consistency (different layouts and patterns between campaigns), clarity (unclear responsibilities or expectations) and technical constraints of the current templates and configuration options.




Conclusion
From Empathize I conclude that the current workflow and template setup do not always support marketers in a simple, predictable and consistent way, and that digital/front‑end colleagues often need to step in with custom adjustments to make pages match the design or campaign needs. The evidence from the five case studies, six stakeholder interviews, satisfaction survey, empathy map and stakeholder analysis shows that people recognize the value of the CMS, but experience friction in areas such as reusing patterns across markets, aligning Figma designs with available components, and handling last‑minute changes without breaking consistency or creating extra work. Overall, Empathize confirms that there is clear room for improvement in the template structure, configuration options, documentation and reuse strategy, and that any new template system should explicitly address the everyday pain points of both marketers (speed, clarity, flexibility) and developers (maintainability, scalability, fewer ad‑hoc fixes).




Next step
Use the insights, pain points and themes from Empathize to define concrete goals, constraints and requirements for a template system in the Define step, so that the next phases of the project are directly grounded in the real problems and opportunities identified in this phase.



Phase 2: Define





Objective
In Define I translate the insights from Empathize into clear goals, constraints and requirements for a new template system that fits both stakeholder needs and the limitations of Delta’s existing custom CMS. The aim is to move from a broad list of issues and opportunities to a structured, prioritised requirement set, using MoSCoW (Must/Should/Could/Won’t) to make the scope realistic.




Linked research question(s) 
Define mainly answers SQ2 – Goals, constraints and requirements for a template system by clustering insights from case studies, interviews, survey and stakeholder analysis into requirement groups (content, structure, usability, self‑service, technical and brand) and validating these with stakeholders.




Approach and methods
I cluster themes and pain points from Empathize into requirement groups such as content structure, page layout, reusability, self‑service for marketers and technical constraints in the CMS. I then perform a requirements analysis, apply MoSCoW prioritisation, validate my interpretation with stakeholders, and review external examples and guidelines to check whether the requirements are realistic and aligned with best practices.




Tasks





Heuristic evaluation





Reason: I perform a heuristic evaluation of current and concept landing‑page layouts against recognised UX and usability principles (for example clarity of calls‑to‑action, consistency, feedback, and mobile friendliness) to turn general observations into concrete usability requirements. This helps me say not only “the current pages feel inconsistent” but also “the template system must support clear visual hierarchy, consistent component behaviour and accessible interaction patterns for both desktop and mobile.”





