Results
The Define step delivers a validated and prioritised list of requirements for the template system, grouped into content and structure needs (for example required sections, modules and content types), usability and self‑service expectations (for example how easily marketers can configure and reuse templates), technical constraints of the existing CMS (for example available fields, components and performance limits), and brand and consistency guidelines that the templates must support. Each requirement is classified using MoSCoW, and the resulting list is reviewed with key stakeholders so that there is shared agreement on what the first version of the template system must deliver and which ideas can be planned for later iterations.




Conclusion
At the end of Define, I have moved from a broad set of pain points and observations to a structured, stakeholder‑validated requirement set that describes what the template system must achieve for its users and what it must respect in terms of technology and brand. The combination of heuristic evaluation, empathy map, personas and Delta landing‑page artefacts ensures that requirements are grounded in real behaviour and real pages, not just in abstract wishes, and that both marketer and developer perspectives are represented in the final MoSCoW list. This gives me a clear, realistic scope for the next phases and reduces the risk of designing concepts that are either technically infeasible or misaligned with stakeholder expectations.




Next step
Based on these requirements, I move into Ideate to explore and compare possible concepts for templates, components and configuration flows, using the Must/Should requirements as hard design criteria and the Could requirements as inspiration for future improvements.



Phase 3: Ideate





Objective
In Ideate I generate and explore different concepts for the template system, including template and component structures, configuration flows and potential AI support options, based on the requirements from Define. The aim is to move from “what the system must do” to concrete ideas for “how it could work in practice”.




Linked research question(s) 
Ideate mainly answers SQ3 - Concept and design of the template system, and explores SQ6 - Potential AI support for configuration at idea level. I develop alternative concepts for templates, components and configuration flows that satisfy the requirements and narrow them down to one coherent concept that fits the existing CMS.





Approach and methods
I start from the requirement groups defined in Phase 2 and translate them into “How might we…?” questions to open up the solution space (for example “How might we let marketers configure pages without breaking consistency?”). I then use a mix of benchmarking, structural analysis and concept sketching: I benchmark other CMSs and template systems, analyse different ways of storing and reusing layouts, study sidebar patterns for configuration, and sketch several alternative flows and page structures. For potential AI support, I review examples of AI‑assisted landing‑page tools and CMS features to see which ideas are desirable, technically plausible and aligned with Delta’s context, keeping them explicitly at “idea level” (no implementation). Throughout this phase I compare concepts against the MoSCoW requirements from Define to avoid “nice ideas” that do not fit the real constraints.




Tasks





Benchmark analysis





Reason: I conduct a benchmark analysis of existing CMSs, landing‑page builders and template systems (for example how they structure templates, components and configuration panels) to learn from proven patterns and common pitfalls. This helps me identify which elements (such as reusable modules, preset layouts, or guided configuration wizards) could be adapted to Delta’s custom CMS and where I need to design something more specific for this organisation.





Benchmark analysis @BenchmarkA

Storage and sharing analysis





Reason: I analyse different ways to store, share and reuse landing‑page templates and components, both within Delta’s current CMS and in external tools. This includes questions such as: How can marketers easily reuse a successful layout? How can we store variations for different markets? How can developers update shared components without breaking existing pages? The outcome is a set of concept options for storage (for example master templates, pattern libraries, or “starting points”) and for sharing templates across teams and campaigns.





Storage and Sharing Analysis @Storage and Sharing Analysis



Left and right sidebar analysis





Reason: I perform a focused analysis of left and right sidebar patterns in template and page builders (for example where navigation sits, where configuration fields live, and how previews are shown) to design a configuration flow that is clear and efficient for marketers. By comparing different sidebar layouts, groupings of settings and preview behaviours, I explore options for how the Delta CMS could support guided configuration without overwhelming users with options.





Left and Right Sidebar Analysis @Sidebars Analysis



AI concept





Reason: I explore high‑level AI concepts that could support marketers and developers when configuring landing pages, such as AI‑generated content suggestions, layout recommendations based on campaign goals, or smart defaults based on previous pages. In this task I map where AI could meaningfully reduce manual work or improve consistency, while staying realistic about data, privacy and technical constraints, so that the AI ideas can inform future development without blocking the current template system.





AI concept @AI-Concept





Results
Ideate leads to one selected concept for the template system that best fits the requirements: a coherent set of templates and components, with a configuration flow (for example using sidebars and clear steps) that supports marketers in building consistent pages with less developer involvement. Alongside this, I produce a short list of promising AI support ideas (such as AI‑assisted copy suggestions or recommended modules per campaign type) that are documented at idea level and explicitly marked as “future options” for Delta’s CMS. The chosen concept is described with its main templates, key components, configuration steps and storage/sharing approach, and is linked back to the MoSCoW requirements from Define.




Conclusion
At the end of Ideate, I know which concept I will take forward into Prototype and why this concept is a good fit for both users and the existing CMS. The benchmark, storage/sharing and sidebar analyses give me evidence that the chosen structure aligns with common best practices while still respecting Delta’s technical constraints, and the AI concept work clarifies which AI ideas are attractive but out of scope for immediate implementation. This phase therefore narrows a broad set of ideas into a grounded, realistic design direction that is traceable back to user needs and requirements, reducing the risk of rework in the later Prototype and Implement phases.




Next step
Turn the chosen concept into a working prototype and implementation in the CMS in the Prototype step, so that I can test the template system with real content, real users and real technical constraints, and refine it based on concrete feedback instead of only theoretical reasoning.



Phase 4: Prototype





Objective
Design and build a first working version of the template system in the custom CMS, including at least one landing‑page archetype, three to five reusable components, and a clearer configuration flow based on the selected concept from Ideate. In parallel, I prototype a small AI support concept if feasible, so I can test how AI might assist with configuration in a realistic but limited way without risking the stability of the core template system.




Linked research question(s)





SQ3 – Concept and design of the template system (detailed design).



SQ4 – Implementation and refinement in the CMS.



SQ6 – Potential AI support for configuration (prototype level).
In Prototype I refine the concept from Ideate into detailed interaction and visual design, translate this design into a working implementation in the CMS, and, where possible, create a small AI prototype that demonstrates one or two concrete support scenarios. This phase therefore connects design decisions (SQ3) with real technical implementation (SQ4) and moves AI support ideas from abstract to tangible example (SQ6).





Approach and methods
I start with low‑fidelity prototypes to define and test the structure, flow and key states of the template system without being distracted by visual details, using simple frames and wireframes in Figma that focus on layout, component placement and configuration steps. Once the structure is stable, I move to high‑fidelity prototypes in Figma that include real UI components, Delta branding, realistic content and interaction details, so stakeholders can see how the system will look and behave. Based on the high‑fidelity design, I implement the archetype page and components in the custom CMS front‑end, keep implementation logs, and document design artefacts (for example component specifications) to make the translation from Figma to CMS explicit and traceable. In parallel, I explore a small AI prototype (for example a guided suggestion or configuration helper) using existing AI tools or scripts, and document its behaviour and limitations clearly.




Tasks





Low‑fidelity prototype





Reason: I first create a low‑fidelity prototype in Figma that captures the basic structure of the landing‑page archetype, the placement of reusable components and the main steps in the configuration flow (for example how a marketer selects an archetype, adds modules and configures content). Working in low fidelity allows me to quickly test different layouts and flows with stakeholders, and to adjust the concept before investing time in detailed visual design or CMS implementation.





