Results
The Prototype step delivers an implemented template system in the CMS with at least one landing‑page archetype and several reusable components that follow the selected concept and the MoSCoW requirements from Define. The configuration flow is now implemented more clearly in the CMS (for example through logical grouping of settings and a more guided sequence of steps), and the Figma prototypes and implementation logs document how the design was translated into code. If feasible, this phase also results in a small AI prototype (such as an example of AI‑assisted configuration or content suggestion) that can be shown to stakeholders as a concrete illustration of how AI might support the workflow in the future.




Conclusion
At this point, the template system is no longer just a concept but a working solution in Delta’s custom CMS, supported by both low‑ and high‑fidelity prototypes that show its structure and visual design. I have demonstrated that the chosen concept can be implemented within the existing technical constraints and that a set of core components and at least one landing‑page archetype can be configured by marketers in a more structured way than before. The implementation logs and design artefacts make the design‑to‑development translation transparent, and the small AI prototype shows, at a realistic but limited level, how AI could support configuration tasks without replacing the core template logic. Together, these outcomes give a solid basis for evaluating the system in real campaigns and for discussing future iterations with both marketing and digital teams.




Next step
Use the new template system and, where relevant, the AI prototype in a real campaign and test their impact in the Test step, focusing on efficiency, consistency, usability and the perceived value of any AI support compared to the previous workflow.



Phase 5: Test





Objective
In Test I evaluate how well the new template system – and, where applicable, the AI prototype – works for stakeholders and how much it improves ease of use, effort and page consistency compared to the previous way of working. The goal is to gather evidence about what works, what creates friction and which changes are needed before finalisation.




Linked research question(s)





SQ4 – Implementation and refinement in the CMS (application in a realistic context).



SQ5 – Impact on ease of use and page consistency.



SQ6 – Potential AI support for configuration (evaluation).
Test uses the implemented system in realistic tasks, compares the new workflow with the old one and evaluates the perceived value of any AI support.





