4. Answers to the sub‑research questions

In this section I provide a concise but explicit answer to each sub‑research question. These answers are based on the outcomes of the Design Thinking phases and bring together the most important findings from the research, design, testing and implementation work carried out during the project.

4.1 SQ1 – Current situation and issues

How are campaign landing pages currently created and configured in Delta’s custom CMS, and what difficulties do marketing teams and developers experience with the existing templates and tools?

The research shows that campaign landing pages at Delta are often created through a fragmented process in which briefings, Figma designs, CMS configuration and front‑end implementation are not always closely aligned. In practice, this means that marketers depend regularly on developers to translate campaign ideas into working pages, especially when the existing templates do not support the desired layout or content structure well enough.

Across the five case studies, stakeholder interviews, observations and survey findings, several recurring problems became visible. First, the workflow contains too much manual work and rework, because pages are frequently adjusted case by case instead of being built from a stable and reusable template logic. Second, there is inconsistency between campaigns, because the available templates and components do not always guide users towards the same structure, hierarchy or interaction patterns. Third, the current configuration process is not sufficiently intuitive for marketers, which limits self‑service and increases dependence on digital and front‑end colleagues. Finally, developers experience friction because ad‑hoc fixes and one‑off solutions reduce maintainability and make it harder to scale successful patterns across campaigns.

The answer to SQ1 is therefore that the current landing‑page process is functional but inefficient, inconsistent and too dependent on manual interpretation and technical support. The main issues are not only visual or technical; they are also process‑related, because the system does not yet provide enough structure, clarity and reuse for the stakeholders who work with it.

4.2 SQ2 – Goals, constraints and requirements for a template system

What goals, constraints and requirements do stakeholders have for a template system that should be user‑friendly for marketing teams and support consistent, well‑structured campaign landing pages?

The findings from Empathize show that stakeholders want a template system that improves both usability and consistency without making the CMS more complicated. For marketers, the most important goal is to be able to create and configure campaign landing pages more independently, with clearer guidance and less need for developer intervention. For the digital and front‑end team, the key goal is to reduce one‑off implementations and improve maintainability, scalability and reuse across campaigns.

These needs were translated in the Define phase into a set of functional, UX and technical requirements. At functional level, the system needed to support reusable page structures, clear component options and a manageable number of content variations. At UX level, it needed to offer a clearer configuration flow, understandable labels, better grouping of settings and stronger guidance for users. At technical level, the solution had to fit within the constraints of Delta’s existing CMS, including the available content models, front‑end structure and implementation capacity. In addition, the system had to respect Delta’s brand identity and support consistent visual and structural patterns across campaigns.

The final answer to SQ2 is that the template system must balance four core requirements: it must be usable for marketers, consistent across campaigns, technically feasible within the existing CMS, and maintainable for the team that supports it. These requirements were prioritised with MoSCoW so that the first version remained realistic and focused.

4.3 SQ3 – Concept and design of the template system

Which template and component concepts are feasible within the existing CMS, and what design for a user‑friendly template system best fits the identified requirements?

The concept exploration showed that the most suitable direction was not an open‑ended page builder with unlimited freedom, but a more structured template system built around reusable archetypes, reusable components and a clearer configuration flow. This concept fits Delta’s context because it gives marketers enough flexibility to build relevant campaign pages, while still protecting consistency and maintainability.

Benchmarking, storage and sharing analysis, and sidebar analysis helped identify which patterns from other systems could realistically be adapted to Delta’s CMS. The selected concept therefore combined a defined landing‑page archetype with modular sections and a more guided way of configuring content and layout. This approach reduces ambiguity for users and makes it easier to repeat successful page patterns across campaigns. It also supports better alignment between Figma design, CMS structure and front‑end implementation.

The answer to SQ3 is that the most feasible and appropriate design is a structured template system based on reusable components, clear page archetypes and guided configuration rather than full design freedom. This concept best matches the requirements from SQ2 because it creates a practical balance between user friendliness, consistency and technical realism.

4.4 SQ4 – Implementation and refinement in the CMS

How can the chosen template system be implemented in Delta’s custom CMS and iteratively refined into a stable, reusable product based on stakeholder and usability‑testing feedback?

The project shows that the chosen concept can be implemented step by step through a combination of prototyping, front‑end development, iterative testing and controlled refinement. In the Prototype phase, the selected concept was first translated into low‑fidelity and high‑fidelity designs, after which the core archetype and reusable components were implemented in the CMS. This made it possible to move from a conceptual direction to a working solution within the real technical environment of Delta.

The Test and Implement phases were essential in turning this first version into a stronger and more complete product. Usability testing with nine participants revealed where the configuration flow was clear, where users hesitated and which parts of the system still needed improvement. These findings were then prioritised and translated into concrete refinements in the final implementation round. As a result, the final version is not only technically implemented, but also iteratively improved on the basis of stakeholder feedback and observed user behaviour.

The answer to SQ4 is therefore that implementation works best as an iterative process: concept design is translated into prototypes, prototypes are implemented in the CMS, and the resulting system is then refined through testing, validation and documentation. This process led to a stable and reusable first version of the template system that is suitable for real campaign use.

4.5 SQ5 – Impact on ease of use and page consistency

To what extent does the new template system improve marketing teams’ perceived ease of use and the consistency/structure of campaign landing pages compared to the previous way of working?

The usability tests and stakeholder feedback indicate that the new template system improves the landing‑page workflow in meaningful ways. Compared to the previous way of working, the new system provides more structure, clearer guidance and a more predictable configuration process, which helps users understand how pages should be built and how components should be used. This supports marketers in working with greater confidence and reduces the need for repeated clarification or developer assistance.

The improvement is also visible in page consistency. Because the system is based on archetypes and reusable components, it becomes easier to apply the same structural logic across campaigns instead of reinventing layouts each time. This does not mean that every page becomes identical, but it does mean that variation happens within a more controlled and coherent framework. For the digital and front‑end team, this contributes to better maintainability and less ad‑hoc correction work.

The answer to SQ5 is that the new template system improves both perceived ease of use and structural consistency, although these improvements should be understood within the scope of the project. It is a strong first step rather than a final solution, but the evidence shows that the new workflow supports stakeholders better than the previous one.

4.6 SQ6 – Potential AI support for configuration

Which parts of the campaign landing‑page configuration process are suitable for support by an AI‑based assistant, and what could a small concept or minimal prototype for such support look like?

The project shows that AI support has potential, but only in carefully selected parts of the workflow. The most promising opportunities are not in replacing the core template logic, but in supporting users with tasks such as content suggestions, recommended page structures, smart defaults or guidance during configuration. In other words, AI appears most useful as an assistive layer around the template system rather than as the main driver of page creation.

This is also why AI remained at concept or small‑prototype level in the project. The research and design work made clear that the primary need at Delta was first to establish a stable, understandable and reusable template system. Introducing AI too early would have added complexity to a workflow that still needed stronger structural foundations. At the same time, the exploration of AI ideas helped identify future directions that could become valuable once the template system itself is mature enough.

The answer to SQ6 is therefore that AI support is most suitable for guidance, suggestions and efficiency improvements around configuration, but should be developed only after the core template system is stable. Within this project, AI is best positioned as a future enhancement rather than a central part of the implemented solution.



5. Conclusion

Is the problem solved?

The project shows that the problem has been substantially solved, but not in an absolute or final sense. The original problem was not only that landing pages were difficult to build, but that the whole process around them was too dependent on manual work, unclear structure, and repeated adjustments across marketing and digital teams. Through the full Design Thinking process, I was able to study the current situation, define the core requirements, design a new template concept, build and test a working version, and finally improve it based on feedback. As a result, the new template system is no longer only an idea, but a practical solution that makes the landing-page workflow more structured, more reusable, and easier to handle in Delta’s custom CMS.

The strongest evidence that the problem was solved comes from the fact that the new system responds directly to the main pain points identified in the research. The old process required too many manual decisions, too much back-and-forth, and too many custom fixes for every new landing page. The new solution reduces that complexity by introducing a clearer landing-page archetype, reusable components, and a more guided configuration flow. This means marketers can work with more confidence, while developers spend less time on repetitive adjustments. In that sense, the project solved the core problem of inconsistency and inefficiency in a meaningful way.

At the same time, it is important to be realistic: the problem is solved within the scope of this project, not forever. A template system is never fully finished, because business needs, campaign types, and CMS constraints will continue to change. What I achieved is a strong first version that addresses the most important issues and creates a better foundation for future work. The system is therefore best understood as a step forward from the previous situation, not as the final endpoint of all possible improvements.

What is the result

The final result of this project is a working and approved template system for campaign landing pages in Delta’s custom CMS. It includes a clearer structure for page creation, reusable components that support consistency, and a configuration flow that is easier to understand and use. It was developed through research, tested with stakeholders, and refined based on real feedback, which makes the result more reliable than a concept-only design.

Another important result is that the project created a shared understanding between research, design, and implementation. The case studies, interviews, survey, empathy map, and stakeholder analysis helped me understand what users needed. The Define phase translated those findings into concrete requirements. Ideate helped me compare different concepts and select one direction. Prototype turned that direction into a real working solution. Test showed what worked and what still needed improvement. Implement then brought the final feedback into the system and completed the solution. Because of this, the project result is not only the final CMS template system, but also a documented process that explains why the solution looks and works the way it does.

The project also produced value beyond the template itself. It made the workflow more visible, clarified the priorities for future development, and showed where AI support could be useful in the future without forcing it into the core solution too early. This is important because it keeps the system practical and focused. The result is therefore both a design outcome and a strategic outcome: a better way of working now, plus a clearer direction for what can come next.

Improvements for the future

Even though the project has delivered a strong result, there are still several improvements that could be made in the future. The first area is template expansion. At the moment, the system is focused on the most important landing-page types and components, but additional archetypes and variations could be added later to support more campaign formats, markets, or content goals. This would make the system even more flexible without losing its consistency.

The second area is deeper CMS integration. Some improvements may still be possible in how the template system connects with the existing technical structure of Delta’s CMS. For example, future work could improve the configuration interface, reduce remaining manual steps, or make component reuse even more automatic. If the CMS environment changes later, the system could also be updated to better match those technical changes. This means the solution can keep evolving with the company instead of staying fixed in one version.

The third area is AI support. In this project, AI support was treated carefully and mainly at idea or prototype level. That was the right decision because the main system needed to be stable first. In the future, however, AI could be explored more seriously for tasks such as suggesting page structures, recommending suitable components, checking completeness of content, or helping marketers configure pages faster. If Delta wants to move in that direction, AI should be added in a controlled and useful way, not as a flashy extra feature.

Another future improvement is better documentation and onboarding. Even a good system can be difficult to use if people do not understand how it works. Future work could therefore include clearer usage guidelines, short examples, and onboarding materials for marketers and developers. This would help new users learn the system faster and would reduce the risk of inconsistent use. It would also make the system easier to adopt across teams.

A final improvement area is ongoing testing and refinement. Because campaign needs change over time, the system should continue to be checked against real use cases. New feedback from future campaigns can reveal where the system still feels slow, unclear, or too limited. That feedback can then feed into the next version. In this way, the project becomes part of a continuous improvement cycle rather than a one-time redesign.

Conclusion and future improvements

Overall, this project reached its goal. It solved the main problem of an inefficient and inconsistent landing-page workflow by creating a more structured, reusable, and user-friendly template system in Delta’s custom CMS. It also showed that a good solution does not come from design alone, but from connecting research, requirements, prototyping, testing, and implementation in one clear process. The final system is therefore both practical and evidence-based.

The most important achievement is that the final solution matches real stakeholder needs and real technical limitations. It is not an abstract concept, and it is not overdesigned. It is a realistic improvement that can be used in practice and extended later. That makes the outcome valuable not only for the current campaign work, but also for the way Delta can think about future landing-page development.

If I look forward, I see the project as a foundation for further growth. The next version could include more templates, smoother CMS interactions, better documentation, and possibly carefully designed AI support. These future improvements would not replace the current result; they would build on it. That is the main strength of the project: it creates a system that already works better now, while still leaving space for continuous development in the future.



